{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And his bow remained in strength.</b> This bow, called baleistra in the vernacular — they set it upon a sturdy and strong wood called forka. And he who draws the bow, if he is strong, draws the bowstring with his arms, and his arms bend, because it says 'they hated him, the masters of arrows.' He returned and said, <b>and his bow remained in strength</b> — and thus the verse's meaning: he sat and ruled over them, for his bow was sturdy and strong, as it is written 'to bind his princes at his pleasure.'" "<b>And the arms of his hands were made supple.</b> His arms bent as he drew the bowstring, and he shot his arrows at them. <b>And they were made supple</b> — the language of dancing, hand waving and foot prancing. Another interpretation: <b>and his bow remained in strength</b> — he did not do to his enemies as they did evil to him, but rather strengthened himself and restrained himself with might, like one who treads a bow, and did not repay them evil for evil, but rather showed them a friendly face. <b>And the arms of his hands were made supple</b> — even though he was king, and the bracelets and gold were upon his hand, he did not grow arrogant toward them. And from where did this trait come to him? From the hands of the Mighty One of Jacob." "<b>From there.</b> From that greatness he sustained and provided for the families of Israel." "<b>[From there.</b> From then.]" "<b>Stone.</b> The language of father and family, and the nun is superfluous in it, like the nun in שגעון, עורון, רצון, חרון, רעבון, ערבון."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:19.739554609Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}